@startuml Composite-Graphics-Before
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam nodesep 25
skinparam ranksep 30
skinparam linetype ortho

title GoF Graphics Example \nBefore Composite

note as Book
  <b>The book's own words (p. 163)</b>

  A simple implementation could define classes
  for graphical primitives such as Text and
  Lines plus other classes that act as containers
  for these primitives.

  But there's a problem with this approach:
  <i>"Code that uses these classes must treat</i>
  <i>primitive and container objects differently,</i>
  <i>even if most of the time the user treats</i>
  <i>them identically."</i>
end note

package "the primitives — no common type" {
  class Line {
    - length : int
    + draw(at : Point) : void
  }

  class Rectangle {
    - width : int
    - height : int
    + draw(at : Point) : void
  }

  class Text {
    - content : String
    + draw(at : Point) : void
  }
}

package "the container — a class apart" {
  class Picture {
    - lines : List<Line>
    - rectangles : List<Rectangle>
    - texts : List<Text>
    - pictures : List<Picture>
    + add(l : Line) : void
    + add(r : Rectangle) : void
    + add(t : Text) : void
    + add(p : Picture) : void
    + draw(at : Point) : void
  }
  note bottom of Picture
    ONE COLLECTION PER PRIMITIVE TYPE,
    and one overload of add() for each.

    Every new primitive — Circle, Polyline —
    means another field, another overload,
    and another loop in draw().
  end note
}

class DrawingEditor {
  + drawSelection(items : List<Object>) : void
  + group(items : List<Object>) : Picture
}
note right of DrawingEditor
  The client

  Its parameter is Object, because
  there is no type that means
  "something drawable".

  So it branches:
    if (o instanceof Line) ...
    else if (o instanceof Picture) ...

  ...even though grouping and drawing
  are operations the user applies to
  primitives and pictures IDENTICALLY.
end note

' the four aggregations are the point: one per type
Picture *--> "*" Line
Picture *--> "*" Rectangle
Picture *--> "*" Text
Picture *--> "*" Picture : pictures

DrawingEditor ..> Line : instanceof
DrawingEditor ..> Picture : instanceof

note as Fix
  <b>What Composite changes</b>

  One abstraction, Graphic, that BOTH the
  primitives and Picture implement — so
  Picture keeps a single List<Graphic>,
  add() has one signature, and the editor
  needs no instanceof at all.

  See Composite-Graphics.png.
end note

@enduml
